COMMIT 0adcbedc3784b0d9a480d05c1969311dac35ed40
fix(core): an Error can no longer escape Delivery.apply() from the ingress save

D-049 (d) promises that a save which throws synchronously becomes the same
retryable NACK and "never an escaped exception". Delivery:319 caught
RuntimeException only, so an Error left apply() as a thrown throwable where the
caller expected a Future<Ack> -- no reply, no 5xx, nothing the http component
can map. F-30 settled that "exception" in that sentence means every throwable,
and that D-049 needs no amendment: the proof is inside the same method, where
:296 and :312 already catch Throwable using D-049 (d)'s own words.

Five catches go to Throwable -- the ingress save, SequentialIntake:41 and :47,
and the sync and at-most-once pipeline runs -- plus landAfter's parameter,
without which the widened local does not compile. A RuntimeException | Error
multicatch was refused at the last two sites: it would stop catching the sneaky
InterruptedException an interrupted await throws, which is what D-056 widened
them for in the first place. D-056's row carries that amendment rather than the
change being slipped in; the verifier measured the refusal as mutant M7, killed
by six cases all failing with "apply threw instead of answering".

All nine of the reviewer's mutants were run and all nine killed, with cmp clean
after each restore. The three that earn their keep: narrowing any of the three
save-path sites to the multicatch is killed by exactly one case each, the one
that throws a sneaky checked IOException. Without those three cases the widening
was untested, because all thirteen original cases threw a LinkageError -- which
is FIX-140's trap exactly one task later. The reviewer had predicted that mutant
would survive and recorded plainly that the measurement proved it wrong. The
cases use hand-written delegates rather than the proxy-based helper, because a
java.lang.reflect.Proxy wraps an undeclared checked exception in
UndeclaredThrowableException, a RuntimeException, so they would have passed
against the very mutants they exist to kill.

All three of the first review's FAIL reasons were the architect's text, not the
implementation. The assertion on the NACK text was unreachable -- the case never
advanced the fake clock, and the clock is the only thing that fires a timer -- so
it is now recorded as a documenting assertion rather than acceptance; measured,
the line above it is what kills the mutant. The comments at SequentialIntake:41
and :47 gave a reason that stops being true once Delivery's own catch is widened
in the same commit, and that took with it the premise this task was built on:
widening the Delivery site alone does not leave a hole one frame deeper. F-30 is
corrected a third and fourth time accordingly, and the comments now give
Intake.save's real contract and name the case that pins each.

